ook and Media Studies"]}]}, "created": "2026-04-21@23:18:42.420", "modified": null, "lastSaved": 0, "primaryTeachMethod": "LEC", "faculty": {"code": "ARTSC", "name": "Faculty of Arts and Science"}, "coSec": {"code": "", "name": null}, "department": {"code": "SMC", "name": "St. Michael's College"}, "title": null, "maxCredit": 0.5, "minCredit": 0.5, "breadths": [{"org": {"code": "ARTSC", "name": "Faculty of Arts and Science"}, "breadthTypes": []}], "notes": [{"name": "Course Note", "type": "COURSE", "content": "<p><strong>Timetable Instructions</strong></p><p><strong>LEC0101: Manga and Anime</strong></p><p>This class examines the culture of Manga and Anime from a historical perspective, from manga\u2019s connection to the art of Edo Japan to the influential bodies of work by Osamu Tezuka and Hayao Miyazaki, and manga and anime\u2019s current boom as a global phenomenon. The history of manga and anime culture provides a unique perspective that\u2019s parallel to and independent from Western popular culture, and serves as a counter example of how the process of globalization and soft power manifest through the complex flow of official and unofficial culture. The class will primarily discuss the various theories and historical contexts that gave rise to our contemporary understanding of manga and anime as an artform and a fledgling industry.</p>"}], "cancelInd": "", "subscriptionTtb": false, "subscriptionOpenData": false, "tb1Active": false, "tb2Active": false, "fullyOnline": false, "primaryFull": false, "primaryWaitlistable": false}, {"id": "696f7d2320609159511a65f1", "name": "Social Media & Digital Platforms", "ucName": null, "code": "BMS316H1", "sectionCode": "F", "campus": "St. George", "sessions": ["20265F"], "sections": [{"name": "LEC0101", "type": "Lecture", "teachMethod": "LEC", "sectionNumber": "0101", "meetingTimes": [{"start": {"day": 2, "millisofday": 46800000}, "end": {"day": 2, "millisofday": 54000000}, "building": {"buildingCode": "UC", "buildingRoomNumber